<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Mesopotamia and Iran</p><h1 class="st-title">Ishaqid dynasty</h1><p class="st-meta">1300 CE – 1592 CE (approx.) · capital Fuman</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Sunni dynasty that ruled western Gilan on the Caspian coast from Fuman until Shah Abbas I absorbed it.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> was divided by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sefidrud">Sefidrud</a> river into Biyapas, west of the river, and Biyapish to the east. From around the 14th century the Ishaqids, who claimed descent from the prophet Isaac, ruled Biyapas, with their centres at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fuman">Fuman</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rasht">Rasht</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The Ishaqids were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shafii-school">Shafi'i school</a>, unlike their eastern rivals the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/karkiya-dynasty">Karkiya dynasty</a>, who were Zaydi and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelver-shiism">Twelver Shia</a>; wars between the two halves of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> were frequent. The economy rested on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a> and fishing, and Gilan's silk became valuable to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/safavid-empire">Safavid Empire</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amira-dubbaj">Amira Dubbaj</a>, a ruler of the early 16th century, married into the Safavid family before rebelling.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abbas-i-of-persia">Shah Abbas I</a> conquered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gilan">Gilan</a> in 1592, removing the last Ishaqid ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamshid-khan-of-gilan">Jamshid Khan</a>, and making the province, with its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silk</a>, crown land.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label